Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how they will be used
• purchases.id (purchase_id): links each inventory record to the original purchase so we can see when the stock was first received (FIFO = earliest “purchased” date).  
• purchases.purchased (date): basis for FIFO ordering.  
• purchases.product_id: lets us connect a purchase to the product itself.  
• inventory.location_id: needed only to keep the link between a physical slot and the pick lines.  
• inventory.product_id: identifies the SKU that is physically in that location.  
• inventory.purchase_id: connects the inventory row with its purchase row (therefore, with its received date).  
• inventory.qty: quantity currently available at that location (what the picker can potentially take).  
• picking_line.order_id: optional if we later need to group by order, but not essential for the basic “pick percentage”.  
• picking_line.product_id: the SKU being requested by the picker.  
• picking_line.qty: how much the picker actually tries to pick (the demand that will be compared with on‑hand qty).  
• products.id: product PK, used to get the human‑readable name.  
• products.name: the label we will show in the final result set.

Typical conditions / joins  
– inventory.product_id = picking_line.product_id (same SKU).  
– inventory.purchase_id = purchases.id (bring in the received date).  
– We will order by purchases.purchased ASC to respect FIFO.  
– We may restrict to inventory.qty > 0.  
– We will aggregate by products.name (GROUP BY).

Exploratory SQL snippets  
(The LIMIT 20 clause merely keeps the preview small.  Each query is preceded by a short comment.)

```sql
-- 1. A first look at what SKUs are in the picking lines
SELECT DISTINCT product_id
FROM picking_line
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. A first look at what SKUs exist in inventory
SELECT DISTINCT product_id
FROM inventory
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect a known SKU (6520) in inventory with its purchase links
SELECT inv.id, inv.location_id, inv.product_id, inv.purchase_id, inv.qty
FROM inventory AS inv
WHERE inv.product_id = 6520
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Bring in the purchase date for that same SKU to check FIFO information
SELECT inv.id AS inventory_id,
       pur.purchased,
       inv.qty
FROM inventory AS inv
JOIN purchases AS pur
  ON pur.id = inv.purchase_id
WHERE inv.product_id = 6520
ORDER BY pur.purchased
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. How much of every product is currently in stock?
SELECT inv.product_id,
       SUM(inv.qty) AS on_hand_qty
FROM inventory AS inv
GROUP BY inv.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. How much of every product has been requested in the pick lines?
SELECT pl.product_id,
       SUM(pl.qty) AS picked_qty
FROM picking_line AS pl
GROUP BY pl.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Combine previous two sub‑totals to get a simple pick‑percentage preview
SELECT a.product_id,
       a.picked_qty,
       b.on_hand_qty,
       ROUND(1.0 * a.picked_qty / b.on_hand_qty, 3) AS pick_pct
FROM (SELECT pl.product_id, SUM(pl.qty) AS picked_qty
      FROM picking_line AS pl
      GROUP BY pl.product_id) AS a
JOIN (SELECT inv.product_id, SUM(inv.qty) AS on_hand_qty
      FROM inventory AS inv
      GROUP BY inv.product_id) AS b
  ON a.product_id = b.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Tie the product names to the previous result
SELECT pr.name,
       a.picked_qty,
       b.on_hand_qty,
       ROUND(1.0 * a.picked_qty / b.on_hand_qty, 3) AS pick_pct
FROM (SELECT pl.product_id, SUM(pl.qty) AS picked_qty
      FROM picking_line AS pl
      GROUP BY pl.product_id) AS a
JOIN (SELECT inv.product_id, SUM(inv.qty) AS on_hand_qty
      FROM inventory AS inv
      GROUP BY inv.product_id) AS b
        ON a.product_id = b.product_id
JOIN products AS pr
        ON pr.id = a.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Preview the FIFO order for one product across all inventory rows
SELECT pur.purchased,
       inv.qty
FROM inventory AS inv
JOIN purchases AS pur
  ON pur.id = inv.purchase_id
WHERE inv.product_id = 6520
ORDER BY pur.purchased, inv.qty
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Skeleton of the final FIFO‑aware, overlap‑aware query
--    (not yet computing the overlap, just setting the stage)
SELECT pr.name,
       SUM(pl.qty)                                   AS total_requested,
       SUM(inv.qty)                                  AS total_available,
       ROUND(1.0 * SUM(pl.qty) / SUM(inv.qty), 3)    AS simple_pick_pct
FROM picking_line AS pl
JOIN inventory     AS inv
     ON inv.product_id = pl.product_id
JOIN purchases     AS pur
     ON pur.id = inv.purchase_id
JOIN products      AS pr
     ON pr.id = pl.product_id
GROUP BY pr.name
ORDER BY pr.name
LIMIT 20;
```

These exploratory steps provide all the raw information needed for a final FIFO, “smallest‑qty‑first” overlap calculation and the required average pick percentage by product name.
